{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work-list", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"facets": {}, "total-results": 376733, "items": [{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 14]], "date-time": "2026-02-14T01:16:09Z", "timestamp": 1771031769006, "version": "3.50.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 13]]}, "group-title": "Computer Science and Mathematics", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "MDPI AG", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 13]], "date-time": "2026-02-13T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1770940800000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "accepted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 6]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>This study formulates collaborative large language model (LLM) agents as a Decentralized Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (Dec-POMDP) and optimizes group behavior using centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE). A group-relative policy optimization (GRPO) objective is introduced to jointly optimize solution quality, coordination consistency, and response latency. Experiments are conducted on collaborative writing and collaborative coding benchmarks comprising 6,000 multi-agent episodes with 2\u20134 agents per task. Compared with single-agent and prompt-only collaboration baselines, the proposed approach achieves a 3.1\u00d7 reduction in task completion time, a 19.4% improvement in output consistency, and a 21.7% increase in coding test pass rate, demonstrating effective performance optimization under partial observability.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.20944/preprints202602.1022.v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 14]], "date-time": "2026-02-14T00:52:54Z", "timestamp": 1771030374000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Reinforcement Learning-Augmented LLM Agents for Collaborative Decision Making and Performance Optimization"], "prefix": 